✅ 4. Demonstrate join()

4.1 Definition
🔴 join() is a method of the Thread class.
🔴 It causes the calling thread to wait until the thread on which join() is invoked finishes its execution.
🔴 It is commonly used to enforce the execution order between threads.

Method Signature
🔴 public final void join() throws InterruptedException

4.2 Why Do We Need join()?

Suppose we have:

Thread-A → Downloads a file.
Main Thread → Reads the downloaded file.

Without join():

Main Thread
│
├── Start Download Thread
└── Read File ❌ (Download may not be complete)

This can cause errors because the file may not exist yet.

With join():

Main Thread
│
├── Start Download Thread
├── Wait (join)
└── Read File ✅


4.3 Syntax
thread.join();

4.4 How join() Works Internally

Suppose:

t1.start();
t1.join();

Flow:

Main Thread
      │
      ▼
t1.start()
      │
      ▼
t1.join()
      │
      ▼
Main Thread BLOCKED
      │
      ▼
Thread t1 Executes
      │
      ▼
Thread t1 Finishes
      │
      ▼
Main Thread Resumes

Important:

🔴 join() does not stop t1.
🔴 It pauses the thread calling join(), which is the main thread in this example.

4.5 join(long millis)
Wait only for a specified time.

thread.join(3000);

Meaning:
Wait for at most 3 seconds.

If the thread finishes earlier:
Main thread resumes immediately.

If not:
Main thread resumes after 3 seconds

join(long millis, int nanos)
thread.join(2000, 500000);

Meaning:
Wait for:

2 seconds
500,000 nanoseconds

This overload is rarely used in real-world applications.